1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,855
Session three.

2
00:00:00,855 --> 00:00:02,443
One process, many clients.

3
00:00:02,443 --> 00:00:07,330
The question for this session: how does one box hold ten thousand clients at the

4
00:00:07,330 --> 00:00:07,941
same time?

5
00:00:08,866 --> 00:00:11,084
There are three ways to serve many clients.

6
00:00:11,399 --> 00:00:12,409
Fork per request.

7
00:00:12,409 --> 00:00:15,023
The whole process is copied for each client.

8
00:00:15,023 --> 00:00:18,528
Simple and isolated, but the most expensive per connection.

9
00:00:18,866 --> 00:00:19,967
Thread per request.

10
00:00:19,967 --> 00:00:22,111
Cheaper than fork, and latency drops.

11
00:00:22,111 --> 00:00:25,646
But every thread still costs a stack before any work is done.

12
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:27,488
One loop with many sockets.

13
00:00:27,488 --> 00:00:32,152
With select or epoll, one thread watches every socket and wakes only for the ones

14
00:00:32,152 --> 00:00:33,016
that are ready.

15
00:00:33,016 --> 00:00:34,629
This is the one that scales.

16
00:00:34,933 --> 00:00:36,622
What actually ships is a mix.

17
00:00:36,622 --> 00:00:41,456
Pre-fork or pre-thread a pool of workers, and let each worker run its own select or

18
00:00:41,456 --> 00:00:42,097
epoll loop.

19
00:00:42,097 --> 00:00:45,359
Isolation from the processes, concurrency from the loop.

20
00:00:45,666 --> 00:00:50,391
The cost of fork is a full process image per connection: page tables, file

21
00:00:50,391 --> 00:00:55,244
descriptors and a scheduler entry duplicated, all paid while the user waits.

22
00:00:56,133 --> 00:00:57,416
Now the select server.

23
00:00:57,416 --> 00:00:58,408
Here is the loop.

24
00:00:58,733 --> 00:01:04,038
Each iteration, clear the set, add the server socket, add every client, and track

25
00:01:04,038 --> 00:01:05,544
the highest descriptor.

26
00:01:05,544 --> 00:01:08,032
Then call select with max fd plus one.

27
00:01:08,333 --> 00:01:11,967
If the server socket is marked ready, accept a new client.

28
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:14,852
For every client marked ready, read.

29
00:01:15,533 --> 00:01:20,751
If read returns zero or less, close the client, and delete it in constant time by

30
00:01:20,751 --> 00:01:22,490
swapping in the last entry.

31
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:23,520
Otherwise, echo.

32
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:26,436
And note the last argument to select.

33
00:01:26,436 --> 00:01:27,646
That is the timeout.

34
00:01:27,646 --> 00:01:29,157
NULL means block forever.

35
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,071
Why does select hit a wall?

36
00:01:32,399 --> 00:01:36,609
The fd set is a fixed-width bitmap, one thousand twenty-four bits on glibc.

37
00:01:36,609 --> 00:01:40,875
A descriptor numbered one thousand twenty-four simply cannot be represented.

38
00:01:41,799 --> 00:01:46,885
The second problem: select is linear in the number of sockets, every single call.

39
00:01:47,199 --> 00:01:51,550
With select you rebuild the set each iteration, and the kernel scans every

40
00:01:51,550 --> 00:01:52,196
descriptor.

41
00:01:52,196 --> 00:01:56,487
Ten thousand idle sockets cost exactly as much as ten thousand busy ones.

42
00:01:56,799 --> 00:02:01,232
With epoll, you register each descriptor once, using epoll_ctl.

43
00:02:01,232 --> 00:02:04,046
Then epoll_wait returns only what fired.

44
00:02:04,046 --> 00:02:06,157
Idle connections cost nothing.

45
00:02:06,466 --> 00:02:09,350
Picture sixty sockets, and only three have data.

46
00:02:09,350 --> 00:02:10,732
select walks all sixty.

47
00:02:10,732 --> 00:02:12,654
epoll hands back just the three.

48
00:02:12,999 --> 00:02:16,514
The number to remember is one thousand twenty-four.

49
00:02:16,514 --> 00:02:21,476
It is FD_SETSIZE, it is your default ulimit, and it is the K in C ten K.

50
00:02:21,476 --> 00:02:22,992
One wall, three names.

51
00:02:22,992 --> 00:02:28,643
Other platforms have their own versions of epoll: kqueue on BSD and macOS, IOCP on

52
00:02:28,643 --> 00:02:31,262
Windows, and io_uring on modern Linux.

53
00:02:32,199 --> 00:02:34,545
In production, you stack these ideas.

54
00:02:34,866 --> 00:02:39,742
At startup, fork or spawn a pool of workers, often one per CPU core.

55
00:02:40,066 --> 00:02:42,702
Each worker runs its own select or epoll loop.

56
00:02:42,999 --> 00:02:46,448
SO_REUSEPORT lets the kernel spread new connections across them.

57
00:02:46,733 --> 00:02:50,982
You get crash isolation from the processes, and concurrency from the loops.

58
00:02:51,266 --> 00:02:56,558
This is Apache's event MPM, nginx's master and workers, and the Go runtime, which

59
00:02:56,558 --> 00:03:01,262
multiplexes goroutines onto OS threads with a scheduler on top of epoll.

60
00:03:02,199 --> 00:03:05,114
Timeouts are a contract, and the client has a half of it.

61
00:03:05,399 --> 00:03:07,425
select waits on three sets.

62
00:03:07,425 --> 00:03:09,675
Read fds: new content arrived.

63
00:03:09,675 --> 00:03:12,450
Write fds: the socket allows writing.

64
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:17,776
And except fds: sockets being connected, closed, or raising exceptions.

65
00:03:18,066 --> 00:03:20,026
The last argument is the timeout.

66
00:03:20,026 --> 00:03:24,243
NULL means block forever, so one wedged client can stop the whole loop.

67
00:03:24,533 --> 00:03:28,582
On the client side, always set a deadline, and then retry with backoff.

68
00:03:28,582 --> 00:03:31,034
We will come back to that in session seven.

69
00:03:31,933 --> 00:03:34,500
Now, how does the server run your code?

70
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:38,121
The oldest answer is CGI, the Common Gateway Interface.

71
00:03:38,466 --> 00:03:43,742
For every request, Apache creates a brand new CGI process, with environment

72
00:03:43,742 --> 00:03:45,571
variables and three pipes.

73
00:03:45,866 --> 00:03:48,792
The request body goes to the program's standard in.

74
00:03:48,792 --> 00:03:52,004
Whatever it prints on standard out becomes the response.

75
00:03:52,004 --> 00:03:54,643
And standard error goes to Apache's error log.

76
00:03:54,933 --> 00:04:00,399
In the real source, mod_cgi dot c, the function run_cgi_child creates the child and

77
00:04:00,399 --> 00:04:05,799
attaches the three pipes, with a timeout on each, so a hung script cannot hold the

78
00:04:05,799 --> 00:04:06,787
worker forever.

79
00:04:07,133 --> 00:04:11,892
The cost is that a whole program image is created, run once, and thrown away, on

80
00:04:11,892 --> 00:04:12,903
every single hit.

81
00:04:13,799 --> 00:04:16,176
How does your program see the request?

82
00:04:16,176 --> 00:04:18,803
HTTP headers become environment variables.

83
00:04:19,866 --> 00:04:21,851
The method becomes REQUEST_METHOD.

84
00:04:22,199 --> 00:04:24,568
The query string becomes QUERY_STRING.

85
00:04:24,866 --> 00:04:29,826
A header like User-Agent: curl becomes HTTP_USER_AGENT equals curl.

86
00:04:29,826 --> 00:04:34,119
Upper-case, dashes become underscores, and an HTTP_prefix.

87
00:04:34,466 --> 00:04:37,473
Plus GATEWAY_INTERFACE and SERVER_PROTOCOL.

88
00:04:37,473 --> 00:04:40,619
The code is ap_add_cgi_vars in util_script.c.

89
00:04:40,933 --> 00:04:43,141
The CGI program itself just prints.

90
00:04:43,141 --> 00:04:44,151
Read the method.

91
00:04:44,151 --> 00:04:45,855
For POST, read standard in.

92
00:04:45,855 --> 00:04:47,874
For GET, parse the query string.

93
00:04:47,874 --> 00:04:52,923
Print a Content-Type header, then a blank line, which is the delimiter, then the

94
00:04:52,923 --> 00:04:53,238
body.

95
00:04:53,238 --> 00:04:57,908
Any language that can read environment variables and print can serve HTTP.

96
00:04:57,908 --> 00:05:00,937
That is why the early web ran on Perl and shell.

97
00:05:01,866 --> 00:05:04,153
Two fixes for the cost of CGI.

98
00:05:04,466 --> 00:05:09,355
One fix is to compile the language into the server, as mod PHP or mod Perl do.

99
00:05:09,355 --> 00:05:14,370
No new process per request, but the app now shares an address space with the web

100
00:05:14,370 --> 00:05:16,877
server, so one segfault takes both down.

101
00:05:17,199 --> 00:05:18,990
The fix that won is FastCGI.

102
00:05:18,990 --> 00:05:24,360
Keep a long-lived pool of processes and talk to it over a Unix or TCP socket using a

103
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:25,574
reduced vocabulary.

104
00:05:25,574 --> 00:05:30,625
The setup cost is taken off the request path and paid once at startup, the same

105
00:05:30,625 --> 00:05:31,967
trick as pre-forking.

106
00:05:32,866 --> 00:05:34,515
Here is the architecture.

107
00:05:34,799 --> 00:05:37,389
Thousands of clients, many of them slow.

108
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:44,005
They talk to nginx or httpd, which is user-facing: it absorbs the slow connections,

109
00:05:44,005 --> 00:05:45,895
handles TLS, and buffers.

110
00:05:46,199 --> 00:05:50,135
Behind it, you define an upstream, speaking FastCGI.

111
00:05:50,466 --> 00:05:54,762
To php-fpm: a fixed pool of long-lived workers.

112
00:05:55,066 --> 00:05:58,650
FastCGI is a modified HTTP with less vocabulary.

113
00:05:58,650 --> 00:06:04,848
It carries the same information, method, headers, query and body, but it is binary,

114
00:06:04,848 --> 00:06:06,490
typed and multiplexed.

115
00:06:07,399 --> 00:06:09,454
Let us look at the actual records.

116
00:06:10,533 --> 00:06:13,586
A request begins with FCGI_BEGIN_REQUEST.

117
00:06:13,933 --> 00:06:20,720
Then FCGI_PARAMS carries the variables: REQUEST_METHOD equals POST, SCRIPT_FILENAME,

118
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:23,952
QUERY_STRING, CONTENT_LENGTH, HTTP_HOST.

119
00:06:24,266 --> 00:06:27,273
An empty PARAMS record means the params are finished.

120
00:06:27,599 --> 00:06:33,203
Then FCGI_STDIN carries the body: name equals alice and age equals twenty.

121
00:06:33,203 --> 00:06:34,415
Seventeen bytes.

122
00:06:34,733 --> 00:06:38,158
And an empty STDIN record means stdin is finished.

123
00:06:38,466 --> 00:06:44,287
The response comes back as FCGI_STDOUT: headers, a blank line, and the body, then an

124
00:06:44,287 --> 00:06:45,188
empty STDOUT.

125
00:06:45,533 --> 00:06:51,136
STDERR goes to the log, in its own record type so debug output can never corrupt the

126
00:06:51,136 --> 00:06:51,469
page.

127
00:06:51,469 --> 00:06:52,603
Then END_REQUEST.

128
00:06:52,933 --> 00:06:54,119
The framing is both.

129
00:06:54,119 --> 00:06:59,040
Every record header carries its content length, and an empty, length zero record is

130
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:01,768
the delimiter that says a stream has finished.

131
00:07:02,666 --> 00:07:06,021
The same idea shows up in the Java world, as servlets.

132
00:07:06,333 --> 00:07:10,768
Jetty and Tomcat are the web server, and the JVM is already running your code.

133
00:07:11,066 --> 00:07:13,185
A thread pool is created at startup.

134
00:07:13,185 --> 00:07:14,479
That is pre-threading.

135
00:07:14,799 --> 00:07:18,176
Isolation is by class loader and thread, not by process.

136
00:07:18,176 --> 00:07:22,578
That is cheaper than fork, but a runaway servlet can hurt its neighbours.

137
00:07:22,866 --> 00:07:28,357
CGI, FastCGI, servlets, and every modern framework answer one question: how do we

138
00:07:28,357 --> 00:07:32,967
run user code for each request without creating the world each time?

139
00:07:33,866 --> 00:07:35,713
Is everything a web server?

140
00:07:35,713 --> 00:07:36,397
Not quite.

141
00:07:37,466 --> 00:07:42,725
Plenty of things speak HTTP: a router's admin page, most Node apps.

142
00:07:43,066 --> 00:07:45,469
Firestore and CouchDB over REST.

143
00:07:45,799 --> 00:07:52,198
But Redis speaks RESP, MySQL has its own binary wire protocol, and MQTT brokers,

144
00:07:52,198 --> 00:07:55,877
messaging backends, and Kafka natively do too.

145
00:07:56,199 --> 00:07:57,412
HTTP is stateless.

146
00:07:57,412 --> 00:08:03,006
Any box can answer any request, a load balancer can send the next request anywhere,

147
00:08:03,006 --> 00:08:05,499
so horizontal scaling is nearly free.

148
00:08:05,799 --> 00:08:10,312
A database wants to stay connected, because the TCP connect, handshake,

149
00:08:10,312 --> 00:08:13,300
authentication and session setup are expensive.

150
00:08:13,599 --> 00:08:18,546
So the question becomes: how many persistent connections can one box hold, not how

151
00:08:18,546 --> 00:08:20,055
many requests per second.

152
00:08:20,999 --> 00:08:22,904
Every server hits four walls.

153
00:08:23,199 --> 00:08:24,569
First, file descriptors.

154
00:08:24,569 --> 00:08:29,132
A connection is a descriptor, and the default limit is one thousand twenty-four.

155
00:08:29,466 --> 00:08:30,312
Second, memory.

156
00:08:30,312 --> 00:08:34,203
A stack per thread and a buffer per connection, paid before any work.

157
00:08:34,533 --> 00:08:36,062
Third, context switches.

158
00:08:36,062 --> 00:08:40,013
Every read, write and system call crosses the kernel boundary.

159
00:08:40,333 --> 00:08:41,725
And fourth, the queue.

160
00:08:41,725 --> 00:08:44,445
Latency and throughput are not independent.

161
00:08:44,445 --> 00:08:45,964
Being slow costs memory.

162
00:08:46,866 --> 00:08:49,374
The history of this problem is two acronyms.

163
00:08:50,666 --> 00:08:55,880
In nineteen ninety-nine, Dan Kegel asked: can one box hold ten thousand simultaneous

164
00:08:55,880 --> 00:08:56,625
connections?

165
00:08:56,625 --> 00:08:58,549
At the time, the answer was no.

166
00:08:58,866 --> 00:09:03,299
In twenty thirteen, Robert Graham argued the kernel itself is the problem, and that

167
00:09:03,299 --> 00:09:05,542
you should bypass it to reach ten million.

168
00:09:05,866 --> 00:09:09,989
Today, two to three million connections have been demonstrated on one server.

169
00:09:09,989 --> 00:09:13,041
And the default ulimit is still one thousand twenty-four.

170
00:09:13,933 --> 00:09:15,602
Memory is the second wall.

171
00:09:15,602 --> 00:09:18,043
Compare an OS thread with a goroutine.

172
00:09:18,333 --> 00:09:21,488
An OS thread reserves an eight megabyte stack up front.

173
00:09:21,488 --> 00:09:25,160
Ten thousand threads is about eighty gigabytes of address space.

174
00:09:25,466 --> 00:09:29,006
A goroutine starts with a two kilobyte stack, grown on demand.

175
00:09:29,006 --> 00:09:31,062
That is four thousand times smaller.

176
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:36,070
The Go runtime multiplexes goroutines onto a few OS threads with a netpoller built

177
00:09:36,070 --> 00:09:36,583
on epoll.

178
00:09:36,583 --> 00:09:40,513
You write simple blocking reads, and the runtime parks you on a poll.

179
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:43,454
The third wall is the kernel boundary.

180
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:47,108
Each trip from user mode into the kernel and back saves state,

181
00:09:47,400 --> 00:09:48,816
switches page tables,

182
00:09:49,133 --> 00:09:50,561
validates arguments,

183
00:09:50,866 --> 00:09:51,911
copies data,

184
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:53,279
and returns.

185
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:58,540
Each one costs hundreds of nanoseconds, and the Spectre and Meltdown mitigations

186
00:09:58,540 --> 00:09:59,404
made it worse.

187
00:09:59,733 --> 00:10:00,913
Two responses.

188
00:10:00,913 --> 00:10:07,321
Bypass the kernel entirely with user-space TCP: DPDK, netmap, mTCP, Seastar.

189
00:10:07,321 --> 00:10:12,295
You lose all your usual tools, but you get the raw packets.

190
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:13,777
Or make fewer calls.

191
00:10:13,777 --> 00:10:18,543
sendfile moves a file to a socket without a user-space copy, and io_uring submits

192
00:10:18,543 --> 00:10:21,133
batches of operations through a shared ring.

193
00:10:22,066 --> 00:10:24,111
Little's Law ties it all together.

194
00:10:24,111 --> 00:10:26,757
Concurrency equals throughput times latency.

195
00:10:27,066 --> 00:10:31,780
Connections in flight equals requests per second, times seconds per request.

196
00:10:32,066 --> 00:10:37,045
At ten thousand requests per second, with a latency of one hundred milliseconds, you

197
00:10:37,045 --> 00:10:38,823
hold one thousand connections.

198
00:10:39,133 --> 00:10:43,719
Same traffic, but with a latency of one second, and you hold ten thousand.

199
00:10:44,066 --> 00:10:45,544
Slowness is a memory bug.

200
00:10:45,544 --> 00:10:50,511
Only getting faster reduces what you hold, so take high-latency work off the request

201
00:10:50,511 --> 00:10:52,344
path, and do it asynchronously.

202
00:10:53,266 --> 00:10:55,391
Now the servers you actually use.

203
00:10:55,391 --> 00:10:56,228
Apache first.

204
00:10:56,228 --> 00:11:01,444
Its concurrency model is called an MPM, a Multi-Processing Module, and it evolved

205
00:11:01,444 --> 00:11:02,345
through three.

206
00:11:02,666 --> 00:11:05,887
Prefork: a pool of processes, one connection each.

207
00:11:05,887 --> 00:11:10,526
Bulletproof, works with non-thread-safe modules, and the most expensive.

208
00:11:10,866 --> 00:11:13,266
Worker: processes each running many threads.

209
00:11:13,266 --> 00:11:17,302
Cheaper, but a thread is pinned to a connection even while it sits idle on

210
00:11:17,302 --> 00:11:17,902
keep-alive.

211
00:11:18,200 --> 00:11:22,883
Event: a listener thread per process parks idle keep-alive sockets on epoll, and

212
00:11:22,883 --> 00:11:25,284
hands workers only the sockets with work.

213
00:11:25,284 --> 00:11:29,148
It arrived around twenty twelve, and is the default on Unix today.

214
00:11:30,066 --> 00:11:32,609
HAProxy arrived in two thousand one.

215
00:11:32,933 --> 00:11:34,794
It was largely select at first.

216
00:11:34,794 --> 00:11:39,779
Epoll landed in Linux two point five point forty-four in late two thousand two, and

217
00:11:39,779 --> 00:11:44,763
HAProxy became multi-threaded only in version one point eight, in twenty seventeen.

218
00:11:45,066 --> 00:11:49,447
Its big idea was to make the load balancer a tier of its own, sitting in front of

219
00:11:49,447 --> 00:11:51,719
the app servers and choosing between them.

220
00:11:52,066 --> 00:11:56,545
A single event-driven process could saturate a network card, and it absorbs

221
00:11:56,545 --> 00:12:01,203
connection churn by keeping a pool of persistent connections to the upstreams.

222
00:12:02,133 --> 00:12:06,696
Varnish, from two thousand six, was the first popular caching reverse proxy.

223
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,479
Squid cached for the client.

224
00:12:08,479 --> 00:12:11,331
It was a forward proxy near the users, on the way out.

225
00:12:11,331 --> 00:12:14,976
Varnish put the same mechanism in front of the origin, on the way in.

226
00:12:15,266 --> 00:12:19,788
Internally, an acceptor thread per listening point, and a worker thread per active

227
00:12:19,788 --> 00:12:24,200
connection, with pools that default to a minimum of one hundred and a maximum of

228
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:24,972
five thousand.

229
00:12:25,266 --> 00:12:30,228
Idle descriptors are handed to a waiter, using epoll on Linux and kqueue on BSD.

230
00:12:30,228 --> 00:12:32,522
Threads for work, a loop for waiting.

231
00:12:32,866 --> 00:12:38,112
Fastly's edge is built on Varnish, and VCL, the Varnish Configuration Language, is

232
00:12:38,112 --> 00:12:41,887
how you configure that two thousand six program even today.

233
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:47,107
And nginx, from two thousand four, built on epoll and sendfile.

234
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:51,888
With read and write, bytes go from disk, to the kernel, to your buffer, back into

235
00:12:51,888 --> 00:12:53,772
the kernel, and out to the socket.

236
00:12:53,772 --> 00:12:57,872
With sendfile, the kernel moves them straight from the file to the socket.

237
00:12:57,872 --> 00:12:59,312
You never touch the bytes.

238
00:12:59,312 --> 00:13:00,310
That is zero copy.

239
00:13:00,600 --> 00:13:05,369
nginx runs one master process and one single-threaded worker per CPU core.

240
00:13:05,369 --> 00:13:09,365
Each worker runs one epoll loop over thousands of connections.

241
00:13:09,666 --> 00:13:14,535
It started as a reverse proxy, and was then abused for caching, load balancing, TLS

242
00:13:14,535 --> 00:13:16,295
termination and rate limiting.

243
00:13:16,295 --> 00:13:18,583
It became the default shape of an edge.

244
00:13:18,866 --> 00:13:23,122
Since version one point seven point eleven, it has a thread pool for disk reads,

245
00:13:23,122 --> 00:13:27,538
because a page fault on a cache miss would otherwise stall every connection on that

246
00:13:27,538 --> 00:13:27,910
worker.

247
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:29,786
Side by side.

248
00:13:30,866 --> 00:13:34,844
Apache, nineteen ninety-five: prefork, worker, event.

249
00:13:34,844 --> 00:13:38,746
It popularised modules and CGI to run anyone's code.

250
00:13:38,746 --> 00:13:40,247
Akamai started here.

251
00:13:40,533 --> 00:13:43,787
HAProxy, two thousand one: select then epoll.

252
00:13:43,787 --> 00:13:46,245
The load balancer as its own tier.

253
00:13:46,533 --> 00:13:51,600
Varnish, two thousand six: a thread per active connection with an epoll waiter.

254
00:13:51,600 --> 00:13:54,486
The caching reverse proxy, now Fastly's edge.

255
00:13:54,800 --> 00:13:58,979
nginx, two thousand four: a master plus one worker per core.

256
00:13:58,979 --> 00:14:01,487
Sendfile, and the answer to C ten K.

257
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:06,792
All four ended up in the same place: an event loop, and a small pool of workers.

258
00:14:07,733 --> 00:14:09,799
Four ideas to keep from this session.

259
00:14:10,133 --> 00:14:10,482
One.

260
00:14:10,482 --> 00:14:12,139
Amortise the setup.

261
00:14:12,139 --> 00:14:16,240
Pre-fork, pre-thread, FastCGI, pools, servlets.

262
00:14:16,533 --> 00:14:16,791
Two.

263
00:14:16,791 --> 00:14:18,788
Nothing is free per connection.

264
00:14:18,788 --> 00:14:23,557
A descriptor, a stack, a buffer and a scheduler entry, times ten thousand.

265
00:14:23,866 --> 00:14:24,239
Three.

266
00:14:24,239 --> 00:14:26,539
Framing is still length or delimiter.

267
00:14:26,539 --> 00:14:30,205
FastCGI uses a length in each record, plus an empty record.

268
00:14:30,533 --> 00:14:30,848
Four.

269
00:14:30,848 --> 00:14:33,621
Concurrency equals throughput times latency.

270
00:14:33,621 --> 00:14:36,268
Only getting faster reduces what you hold.

271
00:14:37,200 --> 00:14:40,671
Here is what the quiz is most likely to ask from session three.

272
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:00,669
That is session three.

273
00:15:00,669 --> 00:15:04,610
Next up, session four: nginx and the code that explains it.

